version: "1.4"
abstract: >-
  A reproducible topographic test of Don Rosa's 1877 Glasgow shoeshine scene
  against the Ordnance Survey 1:500 plans and the Post Office directories.
  Of 187 configurations on twelve selected facades, 17 on five corners pass
  the author's contested width-ratio model; Jamaica Street and Howard Street NE
  is the only one of those five with affirmative 1877-78 evidence for both
  required trades. The drawn distance is shorter than any admissible opening,
  and the model-read width ratio removes the candidate. Jamaica and Howard NE
  is the strongest candidate and the probable topographic basis of the scene,
  although literal single-source copying is not established. Measuring the
  viewing angles on the plan keeps the same five corners.
